.lua
book.chapter(28, "Lexers and grammar bridges")
.endlua
.sh 2 "Separate lexical decisions from grammar decisions"
.pp
A lexer groups characters into tokens; a parser groups tokens into syntax.
FlexBridge exposes ordered longest-match tokenization inside MetaML, while
YACCBridge accepts supported grammar descriptions and produces generic Ast
values. These are in-process library facilities backed by DSLtk, not shell
invocations of external flex and yacc programs. Their names indicate the
kind of interface, not complete compatibility with every feature of those
external tools.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[rules := [("num", "[0-9]+"), ("_ws", "[ \t\n]+")]
Test.equal "tokens" (FlexBridge.tokenize rules "12 34")
  (Ok [("num", "12"), ("num", "34")])
Test.check "unrecognized byte" (Result.is_err (FlexBridge.tokenize rules "12?"))
Test.equal "prefix match" (FlexBridge.match_prefix "[0-9]+" "123x") (Some "123")]=],
    "Token categories and skipped whitespace")
.endlua
.pp
Rules whose names begin with underscore are skipped. Keep whitespace rules
explicit so the accepted input language is visible. A lexical error should
report where no rule matched rather than silently dropping unknown bytes.
For source tools, use span-producing tokenization when positions matter.
Discarding lexeme locations early makes later grammar and semantic errors
harder to connect to the user's source.
.sh 2 "Longest match and tie order"
.pp
The longest matching rule wins. Earlier rules break ties. This combination
supports a keyword rule before an identifier rule: an exact keyword wins
the equal-length tie, while a longer identifier beginning with that keyword
remains an identifier. Reversing the rules can change the token kind without
changing the lexeme bytes. Rule order is therefore part of a lexer contract,
not a formatting detail that can be sorted alphabetically.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[rules := [("if", "if"), ("id", "[a-z]+"), ("_ws", " +")]
Test.equal "keyword tie and longer identifier"
  (FlexBridge.tokenize rules "if iffy")
  (Ok [("if", "if"), ("id", "iffy")])]=], "Priority applies only after match length")
.endlua
.pp
The supported pattern language includes character classes, simple quantifiers,
escapes, and negative lookahead. It is not unrestricted PCRE. Validate
patterns supplied as configuration before using them in a tokenization
pipeline. Avoid rules that can accept empty input: tokenization requires
progress, and an empty token cannot advance the source cursor. Whitespace
and comment rules need the same progress discipline as ordinary tokens.
.sh 2 "Grammar descriptions establish structure"
.pp
YACCBridge supports productions, alternatives, quoted terminals, and the
tested token conventions of its grammar reader. Check validates a grammar
and returns information through Result; literals inspects its terminals.
Parse consumes source text, while parse_tokens accepts token pairs supplied
by a lexer. Keep semantic actions in a later AST visitor rather than
assuming an arbitrary yacc action language executes inside the grammar.
.lua
book.grammar("An arithmetic grammar design", {
    {"Expr", 'Term { "+" Term }'},
    {"Term", 'Atom { "*" Atom }'},
    {"Atom", 'number | "(" Expr ")"'}
})
.endlua
.pp
The descriptive grammar separates precedence levels. Multiplication is
recognized inside Term before addition combines terms into Expr. A recursive
grammar can express this structure in several equivalent ways, but the
resulting AST shape may differ. Inspect actual parse output before writing
a lowering pass. Do not infer child order from a diagram alone when the
parser retains punctuation tokens or introduces production-name nodes.
.sh 2 "Left recursion and ambiguity"
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[grammar := "expr : expr \"+\" term | term ;\n"
  ^ "term : term \"*\" atom | atom ;\n"
  ^ "atom : NUM | ID | \"(\" expr \")\" ;\n"
Test.equal "grammar vocabulary" (YACCBridge.check grammar)
  (Ok ["expr", "term", "atom"])
Test.check "nested arithmetic"
  (Result.is_ok (YACCBridge.parse grammar "1 + 2 * (x + 3)"))
Test.check "missing operand"
  (Result.is_err (YACCBridge.parse grammar "1 + * 2"))
Test.check "token input"
  (Result.is_ok (YACCBridge.parse_tokens grammar
    [("NUM", "7"), ("OP", "*"), ("ID", "k")]))]=],
    "An executable bridge grammar with source and token entry points")
.endlua
.pp
The bridge supports tested left-recursive grammar forms through its underlying
grammar machinery. That does not mean every parser facility in DSLtk has
the same recursion behavior: ordinary recursive-descent combinators can loop
on direct left recursion. Choose a grammar formulation suited to the parser
being used. When changing the parsing backend, review recursion, alternative
ordering, and error behavior rather than copying a grammar blindly.
.pp
Ambiguity occurs when an input has more than one derivation. A parser returning
one tree has not proved the grammar unambiguous. For arithmetic, encode
precedence and associativity deliberately, then test expressions that expose
them. Addition-only examples cannot distinguish left from right association;
subtraction and division can. A grammar test should challenge the structural
decision it claims to establish.
.lua
book.algorithm("Build a lexer-to-tree boundary", {
    "Validate lexical rules and the grammar description.",
    "Tokenize source while preserving token kinds, lexemes, and positions.",
    "Reject unrecognized input before grammar interpretation.",
    "Parse the token sequence and inspect the produced tree vocabulary.",
    "Lower that vocabulary into a domain model with checked arities.",
    "Attach source context to failures at each boundary."
})
.endlua
.sh 2 "Test failure as carefully as acceptance"
.pp
A useful lexer suite covers ties, longer matches, skipped input, unsupported
characters, and end-of-input boundaries. A grammar suite covers missing
operands, extra tokens, nesting, and precedence. Verify full consumption when
the public operation promises a complete program. A parser that accepts a
valid prefix and ignores trailing garbage is unsuitable for configuration
validation unless prefix parsing is explicitly the intended interface.
.pp
Keep lexical and semantic numeric validation distinct. A token matching
digits may still exceed the Int range. Likewise, an identifier token may
refer to an unknown variable. The lexer should not silently assign a default
numeric value or resolve names merely because it can recognize their
spelling. Each stage establishes the invariant needed by the next, retaining
enough context to explain why a later requirement failed.
.sh 2 "Sources and further work"
.pp
Read metalib/cpp/FlexBridge.h and YACCBridge.h, their included implementation
fragments, and the corresponding D modules. Locate the bridge fixtures in
tests before relying on a particular grammar extension. Design a lexer with
one keyword, identifiers, integers, and whitespace. Test a keyword prefix
inside a longer identifier. Then sketch a precedence grammar and list the
tree shapes its lowering pass must recognize, including punctuation and
error cases.
